---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
// The project has two lineages and one upstream original, so `REPOS` carries
// four links, not one. It is the single source of them (src/data/nav.ts): the
// header, the hero row and the footer all render the same four. The about
// section links the lineage repos by name, so it imports the same constants
// rather than hardcoding URLs.
import { PET_REPO, REPOS, SHELL_REPO, TOOLCHAIN_REPO } from '../data/nav';

const xpTable = [
  { event: 'task_success', xp: '14', when: 'a turn ends successfully, once per turn' },
  { event: 'recovery', xp: '6', when: 'a success after a failure in the same turn' },
  { event: 'task_failure', xp: '5', when: 'a turn fails' },
  { event: 'review_opened', xp: '5', when: 'a review or critique is opened' },
  { event: 'prompt_sent', xp: '4', when: 'every prompt you send' },
  { event: 'care', xp: '3', when: 'you tend the pet yourself' },
  { event: 'session_start', xp: '2', when: 'an agent session begins' },
];

const stages = [
  { name: 'egg', xp: '0', width: 1, colour: 'var(--stage-egg)' },
  { name: 'hatchling', xp: '1,041', width: 1.5, colour: 'var(--stage-hatchling)' },
  { name: 'child', xp: '5,040', width: 5, colour: 'var(--stage-child)' },
  { name: 'teen', xp: '10,006', width: 10, colour: 'var(--stage-teen)' },
  { name: 'adult', xp: '20,158', width: 20, colour: 'var(--stage-adult)' },
];

// Code samples live in the frontmatter and are emitted as string values: in an
// Astro template a raw `{` starts an expression, so JSON written inline would
// be parsed as JavaScript instead of printed.
const gateSnippet = '"evopet": { "evolutionGates": [11, 23, 32, 45] }';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="EvoPet: one pet that grows from all of your agent work"
  description="EvoPet is a pixel-art desktop companion that earns XP from real agent turns across every profile and every agent you run, and evolves on a ladder of 99 levels."
  suffix={false}
>
  <section class="hero">
    {/* One ambient element, maximum: a slow drift behind the pet. */}
    <div class="ambient" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="hero-copy">
        <p class="eyebrow">A pixel-art companion for real work</p>
        <h1>One pet. Every session. Every agent.</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          EvoPet is a pixel-art creature that lives on your desktop and in your agent's terminal,
          and grows from the work you actually do. It is <strong>one pet</strong>: every session,
          in every profile, across every agent you run, feeds the same creature.
        </p>
        {/* §5: two CTAs (the five-button row was the hero's main defect), then
            the four repository links on their own rule below. */}
        <div class="hero-actions">
          <a class="button button--primary" href="/docs#install">Get started</a>
          <a class="button" href="/docs">Browse the docs</a>
        </div>
        <nav class="repo-links" aria-label="Repositories">
          <span class="mono-label">Source</span>
          {
            REPOS.map((repo) => (
              <a
                href={repo.href}
                data-external
                rel="noopener noreferrer"
                aria-label={`${repo.label} on GitHub — ${repo.note} (external)`}
              >
                {repo.label}
              </a>
            ))
          }
        </nav>
      </div>
      <figure class="hero-art">
        {/* The pet itself, from the same one sprite cell the terminal and the
            desktop draw. The stylesheet already had this seat (and the
            reduced-motion rule for it) with no element in it. */}
        <img
          src="/pet-cell-idle-4x.png"
          width="768"
          height="416"
          alt="EvoPet, drawn from its own sprite atlas: one idle cell of the pet at four times the atlas size."
          decoding="async"
        />
        <figcaption>one idle cell of the shipped atlas, 4×</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="wrap">
    <section class="section tint--amber">
      <h2>Two halves, one creature</h2>
      <p class="prose">
        EvoPet joins two lineages at one idea: a PetDex-inspired desktop shell and the
        TamaHermes/TamaCodex pet line. They are separate trees with separate jobs, and both are
        needed for the thing to exist. <strong>EvoPet is not a fork of PetDex</strong> &mdash;
        PetDex is credited as the shell's inspiration and homage, and nothing more.
      </p>
      <p class="prose">
        The pet half descends from <a
          href={TOOLCHAIN_REPO}
          data-external
          rel="noopener noreferrer">TamaCodex</a
        >, the original upstream source for the pet-creation lineage, by way of <a
          href={PET_REPO}
          data-external
          rel="noopener noreferrer">TamaHermes</a
        >, the maintained pet-half predecessor, which continues on its own. The shell half
        descends from <a href={SHELL_REPO} data-external rel="noopener noreferrer">PetDex</a>.
      </p>
      <div class="grid">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>The pet</h3>
          <p>
            The mechanics. It owns XP, the level ladder, the stats, the life stages and evolution,
            and it draws the sprite atlas: a grid of 192&times;208 cells, 8 columns by 9 rows,
            1536&times;1872 in all. Python.
          </p>
          <p>
            Every surface renders that one atlas, so the terminal pet, the desktop pet and the
            preview are the same creature and cannot drift apart.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>The desktop shell</h3>
          <p>
            The app that floats the creature above your other windows and gives it a HUD. It also
            taps the hook payload every hooked agent sends, so the ledger sees Claude Code, Codex,
            opencode and Gemini CLI as well as Hermes.
          </p>
          <p>
            Growth events are read at turn boundaries only. A turn in one agent is worth a turn in
            another.
          </p>
          <p>
            Right-click the pet to open Clean, Feed and Play controls. Tending this way earns care
            XP on the same shared ledger every agent feeds. While an agent waits on an approval or
            a clarification, the pet keeps jumping and waving until you answer or dismiss the
            prompt.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section tint--mint">
      <h2>One pet, not nine</h2>
      <p class="prose">
        This is the idea the whole project exists for, and it is worth stating plainly: you do not
        get a pet per tool. Profiles, group chats and agents are all sources, and they add up in
        one place.
      </p>
      <p class="prose">
        There are two routes into the combined ledger, and each source is counted on exactly one
        of them. Hermes turns arrive from the per-profile ledgers, which know the details the
        plugin can see. Foreign agents arrive from a spool file the desktop shell writes as their
        events pass through it. A Hermes event seen on the spool is ignored, because the profile
        ledger already counted it.
      </p>
      <div class="diagram-scroll">
        <svg
          class="diagram"
          viewBox="0 0 696 300"
          role="img"
          aria-labelledby="onepet-title onepet-desc"
        >
          <title id="onepet-title">How every agent feeds one pet</title>
          <desc id="onepet-desc">
            Hermes profiles feed their per-profile ledgers, which the drain reads. Claude Code,
            Codex, opencode and Gemini CLI post to the desktop shell's hook server, which spools
            them. Both routes land in one combined ledger, which the compiler renders into one
            sprite atlas that the terminal and the desktop both display.
          </desc>

          <text x="12" y="26" fill="#C9BDE4" font-size="13" font-weight="600">sources</text>

          <rect x="12" y="42" width="200" height="34" rx="7" fill="#29204A" stroke="#4A3C78"></rect>
          <text x="112" y="64" text-anchor="middle" fill="#FFF6EE" font-size="14">Hermes profiles</text>

          <rect x="12" y="86" width="200" height="34" rx="7" fill="#29204A" stroke="#4A3C78"></rect>
          <text x="112" y="108" text-anchor="middle" fill="#FFF6EE" font-size="14">Claude Code</text>

          <rect x="12" y="130" width="200" height="34" rx="7" fill="#29204A" stroke="#4A3C78"></rect>
          <text x="112" y="152" text-anchor="middle" fill="#FFF6EE" font-size="14">Codex, opencode, Gemini</text>

          <path d="M212 59 H 250 V 118" fill="none" stroke="#6B5BA8" stroke-width="1.5"></path>
          <path d="M212 147 H 250 V 118" fill="none" stroke="#6B5BA8" stroke-width="1.5"></path>

          <rect x="250" y="98" width="150" height="40" rx="7" fill="#1E1836" stroke="#4A3C78"></rect>
          <text x="325" y="114" text-anchor="middle" fill="#C9BDE4" font-size="11.5">two routes,</text>
          <text x="325" y="129" text-anchor="middle" fill="#FFF6EE" font-size="13">counted once each</text>

          <path d="M400 118 H 440" fill="none" stroke="#6B5BA8" stroke-width="1.5"></path>

          <rect x="440" y="82" width="164" height="72" rx="8" fill="#29204A" stroke="#7BE495"></rect>
          <text x="522" y="106" text-anchor="middle" fill="#7BE495" font-size="14" font-weight="600">one combined ledger</text>
          <text x="522" y="126" text-anchor="middle" fill="#C9BDE4" font-size="11.5">levels, stats, stage, gates</text>
          <text x="522" y="142" text-anchor="middle" fill="#C9BDE4" font-size="11.5">no prompts, no paths</text>

          <path d="M522 154 V 196" fill="none" stroke="#6B5BA8" stroke-width="1.5"></path>

          <rect x="360" y="196" width="324" height="40" rx="7" fill="#1E1836" stroke="#4A3C78"></rect>
          <text x="522" y="214" text-anchor="middle" fill="#FFF6EE" font-size="13">the compiler draws one 1536x1872 atlas</text>
          <text x="522" y="229" text-anchor="middle" fill="#C9BDE4" font-size="11.5">and installs it everywhere the pet appears</text>

          <rect x="12" y="196" width="324" height="40" rx="7" fill="#1E1836" stroke="#4A3C78"></rect>
          <text x="174" y="214" text-anchor="middle" fill="#FFF6EE" font-size="13">terminal prompt and desktop</text>
          <text x="174" y="229" text-anchor="middle" fill="#C9BDE4" font-size="11.5">same creature, same stage, both places</text>

          <path d="M360 216 H 344" fill="none" stroke="#6B5BA8" stroke-width="1.5"></path>
        </svg>
      </div>
      <p class="prose">
        Because the desktop mirror is one pet shown machine-wide, exactly one writer owns it: the
        drain that renders it from the combined ledger. A per-profile build that tries to write
        that same mirror is refused, by name, rather than silently fighting over it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section tint--lemon">
      <h2>How it works</h2>
      <p class="prose">
        Four steps, and none of them need a network or an account. Everything the pet knows is
        numbers on your disk.
      </p>
      <div class="grid">
        <div class="card">
          <h4>Step 1</h4>
          <h3>An agent does something</h3>
          <p>A hook fires: a prompt, a successful turn, a failure, a review, a session start or end.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h4>Step 2</h4>
          <h3>The ledger records it</h3>
          <p>
            The event is applied as XP plus stat changes, at the turn boundary. One turn, one
            entry, whichever agent ran it.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h4>Step 3</h4>
          <h3>The compiler redraws</h3>
          <p>
            The pet's atlas is rebuilt and installed. Rebuilds are coalesced, so a burst of tool
            calls is one redraw, not forty.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h4>Step 4</h4>
          <h3>Every surface renders it</h3>
          <p>The terminal prompt draws the in-cell HUD; the desktop mirrors the same sheet.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section tint--sky">
      <h2>The ladder: 99 levels, 100,000 XP</h2>
      <p class="prose">
        Every pet climbs the same curve, so a level means the same amount of work in every package.
        It is cheap at the start and long at the end:
        <code>xp_for_level(L) = round(100_000 &times; ((L &minus; 1) &divide; 98) &sup2;)</code>.
        The first level costs 10 XP, which one prompt and one success covers with room to spare.
      </p>
      <ul class="ramp">
        {
          stages.map((stage) => (
            <li>
              <span>{stage.name}</span>
              <span class="ramp-bar">
                <span
                  class="ramp-fill"
                  style={`width:${stage.width}%;background:${stage.colour}`}
                ></span>
              </span>
              <span class="ramp-label">{stage.xp} XP</span>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
      <p class="prose">
        Those are the default pet's evolution gates. A gate is a level your pet reaches, and when
        it does, it changes form. The numbers above are the levels and the XP that opens each one.
        Fifty thousand XP, half the cap, lands inside level 70.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section tint--sky">
      <h2>A quiet HUD that stays live</h2>
      <p class="prose">
        The macOS desktop companion keeps a small floating panel beside the pet. Its XP bar measures
        progress inside the current level, and the level number is derived from that same XP value, so
        the two cannot drift after a turn or care action. The panel refreshes when the ledger changes;
        restarting the app is not required.
      </p>
      <div class="grid">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Actions without a menu</h3>
          <p>
            Care, feed, clean, play and bed are available as compact floating buttons. Each action
            writes to the shared ledger, awards the defined XP, updates the pet's metrics, and redraws
            the installed package.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Readable at a glance</h3>
          <p>
            The panel groups the relevant state in one restrained grid: health, energy, mood, bond,
            satiety, mess, focus, resilience, restlessness, alert state, level and XP progress. It is
            information-dense, not screen-filling.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section tint--rose">
      <h2>Evolution is the creator's call</h2>
      <p class="prose">
        EvoPet fixes the ladder. How many times a pet changes form, and at which levels, is
        declared by whoever made it, in the package's own manifest:
      </p>
      <pre><code>{gateSnippet}</code></pre>
      <p class="prose">
        One to four gates, strictly increasing, one more form than gates: so between two and five
        forms. Names are positional and not yours to choose, starting at <code>egg</code> and
        running <code>hatchling</code>, <code>child</code>, <code>teen</code>,
        <code>adult</code>. Three gates and your pet never reaches adult.
      </p>
      <p class="prose">
        The gates are checked against the art the pet actually ships, at install, while the
        mistake is still fixable. You cannot declare five forms for a line that has two.
      </p>
      <p class="prose">
        <a href="/docs#gates">Evolution stages and gates</a> has the full contract, including
        how to pick gates you will not regret.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section tint--mint">
      <h2>What earns XP</h2>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <caption>XP is awarded at turn boundaries, never per tool call.</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">event</th><th scope="col">XP</th><th scope="col">fires on</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {xpTable.map((row) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row"><code>{row.event}</code></th>
                <td>{row.xp}</td>
                <td>{row.when}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
            <tr>
              <th scope="row"><code>rest</code>, <code>idle_minute</code></th>
              <td>0</td>
              <td>these move stats and energy only</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="prose">
        A good turn is about 18 XP, and a failing turn about 9 to 11. Telling a pet to work harder
        than that is not a setting; there is no cap on the ladder and no way to buy a level.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section tint--amber">
      <h2>What you see on the desktop</h2>
      <p class="prose">
        The pet itself carries its progress: the growth bar and level number are drawn into the sprite.
        Right‑click for Clean, Feed and Play. Each writes to the same ledger every agent feeds.
      </p>
      <div class="note note--warn">
        <span class="note-title">Planned, not shipped yet</span>
        <p>
          A separate floating status panel, more care actions, and a package reload without restarting the shell are planned.
          Do not treat them as available.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section tint--amber">
      <h2>Where things stand</h2>
      <p class="prose">
        Said plainly, because the rest of this site is documentation and documentation should not
        shade the truth:
      </p>
      <ul class="prose">
        <li>The pet's mechanics, the combined ledger and the drain that fills it are in the pet tree.</li>
        <li>
          The desktop shell ships as a local build on macOS. The in‑cell HUD (growth bar and level
          in the sprite) is the shipped surface. A separate floating status panel is planned.
        </li>
        <li>
          The library service is written and passes its own end-to-end round trip, but it is not
          deployed. The library page here will say it cannot reach the library, and that is the
          honest answer rather than an empty list.
        </li>
        <li>EvoPet's own repository is public as of 11 September 2026.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="hero-actions">
        <a class="button button--primary" href="/docs#install">Install the pet</a>
        <a class="button" href="/guides">The guides</a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
